{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-d59c8994-1439-4439-a1c1-e4a4c8ae25f9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae7f4062078f6406","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9014-np2t8pyv/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9014-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014: Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n\nStrategy: RHAISTRAT-9014 (Critical priority, Refinement Done).\n\n## Triage\n\nNot below-threshold: the strategy spans three affected components (Llama Stack,\nTrustyAI, Dashboard) plus a documentation uplift stream, owned by three distinct\nteams — architecturally distinct sub-systems warranting separate PR review\ncycles. Not docs-only: substantive code and integration work is required.\nProceeded with full DAG decomposition.\n\n## Strategy Structure Parse\n\nSections present: Problem Statement, High-Level Requirements (HLR-1..HLR-6),\nAffected Components (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard), Out of Scope, Open\nQuestions.\n\nSections missing (health warnings, proceeded with available information):\nImpacted Teams table, Dependencies table, Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then),\nNon-Functional Requirements. Owner teams were derived from the architecture\ncontext files (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`). No\nStaff Engineer Input section exists in the strategy.\n\n## Component Graph\n\nAll three affected components are in the platform (architecture context files\nexist for each), so no onboarding chain is required:\n\n- **Llama Stack** — Active. Tech Preview today (corroborated by\n  `architecture/rhoai-3.0/llama-stack.md`); GA graduation work is the core of\n  the strategy. Owner: RHOAI GenAI. Konflux onboarded.\n- **TrustyAI** — Active. HLR-4 requires the guardrails integration to become a\n  supported, documented integration rather than an experimental flag. Owner:\n  TrustyAI team.\n- **Dashboard** — Active. HLR-5 requires Llama Stack management enabled by\n  default for entitled clusters (remove the TP opt-in gate). Owner: RHOAI\n  Dashboard.\n- **Documentation** — appears via HLR-6 (GA-standard docs uplift with TP\n  migration notes); substantive content authoring, handled as a separate\n  `docs-authoring` epic assigned to the Llama Stack owning team (RHOAI GenAI),\n  since no Impacted Teams table exists to assign a docs team.\n\nComponent name mapping note: all epic `component` values are copied verbatim\nfrom `.context/rhai-components.txt` (`Llama Stack`, `TrustyAI`, `Dashboard`,\n`Documentation`). No non-obvious mappings were needed.\n\n## Step 0/1 Analysis: The Central Unknown\n\nThe strategy's defining characteristic (Problem Statement + Open Question 1) is\nthat the GA gap inventory is unknown: TP feedback is scattered across support\ntickets and no systematic GA-readiness assessment has been done. HLR-1 makes\nthat assessment a P0 deliverable that \"produc[es] the definitive GA scope\".\nPer the Step 3 decision rule, this answer changes what downstream epics do (the\nsize and content of the stability and hardening work), so HLR-1 becomes an\nInvestigation epic gating the scope-defining implementations. The outcome space\nis unbounded (an unknown inventory of gaps), so no conditional branches are\nemitted — the gate is recorded via `gated_by` / `gate_failure_impact` on the\ndownstream epics.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | GA-readiness assessment producing the definitive GA scope | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantee: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Llama Stack production support posture: SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | TrustyAI guardrails as a supported, documented Llama Stack integration | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Dashboard: enable Llama Stack management by default for entitled clusters | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift with TP migration notes | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n\nE006 priority note: per the docs-authoring priority exception, E006's priority\nis derived from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0, not from\nHLR-6's P2 label, and it does not push priority upstream onto the\nimplementations it depends on.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 GA-readiness assessment] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 API stability]\n    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 Support posture]\n    E002 --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 TrustyAI supported integration]\n    E002 --> E006[RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 GA docs uplift]\n    E003 --> E006\n    E004 --> E006\n    E005[RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 Dashboard default enablement] --> E006\n```\n\nCritical path: E001 → E002 → E004 → E006 (4 epics).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The GA scope defined by the assessment determines the size and content of the API stability and hardening work; Open Question 1 states the work size \"is an estimate\" until it lands. True gate: `rewrite` — assessment findings (e.g. additional unstable API surfaces, upgrade-path gaps) change what E002 must do. |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The assessment's scale-limit and error-handling findings feed the supported configuration matrix and SLO targets; the GA bar the posture must meet is defined by the assessment. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer serial) | The TrustyAI guardrails integration builds against the Llama Stack API surface; a supported integration must target the published, versioned, stability-guaranteed API and deprecation policy rather than the TP surface it replaces. |\n| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | GA documentation must describe the versioned API surface, deprecation policy, and upgrade path that E002 delivers. |\n| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | GA documentation must reflect the published SLO targets, backport policy, and supported configuration matrix. |\n| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | The TrustyAI guardrails integration must be documented as supported; docs describe what was built. |\n| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation must cover default enablement for entitled clusters (removal of the TP opt-in gate). |\n\nDeliberately absent edges:\n\n- **E001/E002/E003 → E005**: the Dashboard default-enablement change is an\n  independent code change in a different repo (feature-flag default in\n  `OdhDashboardConfig`); the GA-milestone timing of flipping the default is a\n  release-coordination point, not an artifact dependency (Rule 14/15). Kept\n  parallel.\n- **E001 → E004**: HLR-4 is a self-defined deliverable (promote the existing\n  experimental TrustyAI integration to supported); its scope does not change\n  based on the Llama Stack gap inventory. Not gated by the investigation.\n- **E003 ∥ E002**: same team and component, but distinct work-product\n  surfaces (code/API hardening vs. support-process/policy artifacts); both are\n  gated by E001 and can proceed in parallel once the GA scope lands. No\n  serializing edge (Rule 15).\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | GA-readiness assessment; the Investigation itself is the deliverable. Remediation items it surfaces are absorbed into E002/E003 scope via their `gate_failure_impact` (add/rewrite). |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path from last two TP releases. |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix. |\n| HLR-4 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | TrustyAI guardrails as supported, documented integration. |\n| HLR-5 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Dashboard default enablement for entitled clusters. |\n| HLR-6 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | (bundled into a single docs-authoring epic at strategy-level Critical priority per the docs-authoring priority exception) — GA docs uplift and TP migration notes are one documentation work stream over one docs surface; not separately schedulable from the rest of the docs uplift. |\n\nAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. There is no external upstream dependency with uncertain\ntiming: all work is in platform-owned midstream repos, and the sole open\nquestion (the GA scope) is resolved by an internal Investigation, not an\nexternal party. HLR-4/HLR-5 (P1) naturally deliver partial value ahead of the\nfull GA bar and are not blocked by the P0 chain.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections**: no Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table,\n  Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, or NFRs. Teams were derived from\n  architecture context; epic acceptance criteria were derived from HLR text.\n  Human verification of team assignments recommended.\n- **Unbounded gate**: the E001 assessment outcome is unbounded (unknown gap\n  inventory). If it surfaces substantial remediation beyond E002/E003 scope\n  (e.g. large-scale error-handling or scale-limit rework), the decomposition\n  should be revisited and new implementation epics spawned — recorded as\n  `gate_failure_impact.action=add_remediation` semantics on the gated epics.\n- **Priority inversion watch**: E006 carries P0 (strategy-level Critical via\n  the docs-authoring exception) even though its underlying HLR-6 is P2. This\n  is per-spec, not an error, but reviewers should not read it as docs work\n  blocking the GA bar.\n- **Coordination (not dependency)**: E005's flag flip should land at the GA\n  release boundary; this is release-train coordination, deliberately not a DAG\n  edge.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":27868,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aacd8cd5f20921ac","agent_id":"aea554450e8b70b1d","session_id":"39ecee98-99e4-452a-b2b9-dea59d7dddfe","uuid":"1f018ec4-a3f8-44cf-9220-a8064fdb1bcb","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:06:50.553Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9014"}
