g and scale-limit gaps ... on the API surface,\" but E002's Scope\nonly commits to remediating API-stability and upgrade-path gaps (E003\nanalogously folds only support-posture gaps). Ownership of error-handling and\nscale-limit code remediation rests solely on the `gate_failure_impact`\nrewrite/spawn semantics rather than an explicit scope bullet — the traceability\nrow overclaims relative to the scope section.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\nNo cycles; no invalid edges. Cross-check: every diagram edge (E001→E002,\nE001→E003, E002→E004, E002/E003/E004/E005→E006) has a matching frontmatter\n`dependencies` entry in the target epic, and vice versa — no extras, no\nmismatches; arrows drawn dependency→dependent per convention. All edges map to\nconstruction rules (Rule 3 investigation gates, Rule 12 framework→consumer,\nRule 11 docs-last). Deliberately absent edges are each justified: E005 kept\nparallel (different repo, release coordination is not an artifact dependency),\nE001 not gating E004 (self-defined HLR-4 scope), E002 ∥ E003 (distinct\nwork-product surfaces, both gated by E001). No konflux-onboarding,\nlicense-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism\nconstraints are vacuous. No branch files. Critical path E001→E002→E004→E006\n(length 4) is reasonable for a 3-component, 6-HLR strategy. Cross-check of epic\ncontent found no data/artifact dependency missing from the DAG (E004's\nconsumption of the versioned API is the E002→E004 edge; E006 consumes all four\nimplementations via its four inbound edges).\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nComponent/team tuples are clean: E001–E003 (Llama Stack / RHOAI GenAI),\nE004 (TrustyAI / TrustyAI), E005 (Dashboard / RHOAI Dashboard), E006\n(Documentation / RHOAI GenAI) — all canonical component names, teams match the\narchitecture-context owner fields. No epic bundles multiple teams. E002/E003\nsplit along work-product axes (code/API hardening vs support-process policy)\nwith the parallelism explici